MEASURED with libinsn. All figures are user-mode only; kernel cost is not measured.

Stock on (b), tapp none:
- idle 0 ms: 132,413 total instructions;
- idle 1000 ms: 251,765;
- so the idle timer thread costs about 119,350 user instructions per second, about 135 per wakeup at the roughly 885 wakeups/s QEMU achieves.

On (a), vCPU1 (the timer thread) ran 894 instructions at idle 0, 133,058-133,354 at idle 1000 and 265,370-265,814 at idle 2000, which is about 132,500 per second.

v2 on (b):
- 149,209 at idle 0 and 149,225 at idle 1000, so about 16 instructions per idle second;
- the one-time cost is about 16.8k instructions (dlsym and the hooks);
- per-call overhead on the non-timer nanosleep path is one TLS test.

v1 on (a): the timer thread ran 1,275 instructions whether idle was 0, 1000 or 2000 ms.

What dominates on the board (only the board can price it): per wakeup, an hrtimer arm through an SBI ecall (no Sstc), a timer IRQ and two context switches, with kernel text executing XIP from flash. docs/current-state.md:6114 records a 157 us switch. The user-mode 135 instructions are negligible next to that.
